/**
\page org_mitk_views_pixelvalue The Pixel Value View

\imageMacro{PixelValueIcon.png,"Icon of the Pixel Value Plugin.",2.00}

The pixel value plugin displays the value of the currently selected pixel.
To avoid ambiguity, is also shows the name of the image of which the pixel value is sourced.
Values of images with integer pixel types are shown as whole numbers; all other values are shown with two decimals, or in scientific notation for very large or small values.

The "All pixel values" section lists every visible image and segmentation at the selected position, from the top-most to the bottom-most layer.
Each row shows a color swatch, the name, and the pixel value.
The swatch approximates the color the pixel is displayed with, including its transparency; a checkerboard shines through for transparent colors.
For multi-component images, the value of the displayed component is shown.
Segmentations get one row per label at the position, named after the segmentation and the label, with the label value as value.
Hidden images and hidden labels are left out, as are segmentations and binary images without a label at the position.

The raw coordinates of the sourced pixel can be optionally displayed.
These coordinates are shown both as indices into the in-memory image array and as absolute world coordinates in millimeters (mm).
For time-resolved images, the current time step is displayed separately.

Note that the in-memory representation or indexing order may differ from that of the image file on disk.
For example, some image readers always use a left-posterior-superior order for the in-memory layout.

Coordinates can be easily copied to the clipboard in comma-separated format.

*/
